		{
			"id": "111931850535493765",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mas.to/@kickstink/111931850516940866",
			"author": {
				"name": "Kickstink",
				"url": "https://mas.to/@kickstink",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-14T21:01:36.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> we already passed the point long ago where it was even possible for a small/single person team to take standards and accessibility seriously (properly seriously) and be able to ever actually ship anything and (the products and ourselves) stay up to date. And you can’t solve it by shouting at your boss, everyone over and under you is owned by the same shitty constraints.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111933807979822079",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://kind.social/@alexiswatson/111933807843061826",
			"author": {
				"name": "Alexis Watson",
				"url": "https://kind.social/@alexiswatson",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/111/933/807/872/112/183/original/c536022662fa9bf9.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-15T05:19:22.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I'm not so arrogant as to claim to have a *good* answer. But:</p><p>I think it has to start with the people who make decisions about tooling to begin with, junior to principal. Revisiting why standards exist, who they serve, and (re)framing the conversation in those terms. </p><p>Heads start to turn when stakeholders ask questions like, \"what do you mean we could have up to 25% more reach with an accessible website?\"</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111933827917786130",
			"parentId": "111933807979822079",
			"url": "https://kind.social/@alexiswatson/111933827892291564",
			"author": {
				"name": "Alexis Watson",
				"url": "https://kind.social/@alexiswatson",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/111/933/807/872/112/183/original/c536022662fa9bf9.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-15T05:24:28.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I'd go a step further and suggest that changing hiring practices goes a long way. Test for what helps the org, the user, and the Web.</p><p>We disallow frameworks in our standard exercise, and test for raw fundamentals, resourcefulness, and expressiveness. Results have been great so far.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111934419366770027",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@hawkticehurst/111934419353110039",
			"author": {
				"name": "Hawk Ticehurst",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@hawkticehurst",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/382/229/410/239/377/original/4b2b3ec1efe031b4.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-15T07:54:53.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> my bet is huge cultural change would happen if:</p><p>1) more content about these tool-chains was created that was tuned/catered towards what's cool/popular (think how HTMX memed their way to being relevant)<br>2) a hugely successful/profitable business was built using tools, techniques, and frameworks aligned with web standards</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111937072059159956",
