		},
		{
			"id": "111939149811873380",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://toot.cafe/@bryanwood/111939149724378006",
			"author": {
				"name": "bryanwood",
				"url": "https://toot.cafe/@bryanwood",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/350/951/934/254/707/original/aa27c1058461a36f.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-16T03:57:53.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Running into this when hiring juniors :( they come in having only done react at previous job or school and have little distinction between it and the web. </p><p>Their first assignments the web.dev learning material to get a better grounding and shake off some of that weird react mental model.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111942650943443282",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://tech.lgbt/@wesruv/111942650314895912",
			"author": {
				"name": "wesruv",
				"url": "https://tech.lgbt/@wesruv",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/524/352/513/111/090/original/fb58bff440e704e0.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-16T18:48:08.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>I think a big part of this (that really bothers me) is web dev is becoming McDonalds/assembly line work.</p><p>We have bootcamps that can train in React (or similar), businesses that will overstaff in that expertise, and then every problem becomes a React problem.</p><p>It's not intrinsically *bad*, on some level  it's easier to get large output this way.</p><p>The problem to me is this approach to web dev and running a business is being evangelized as the best way, and we have massive overuse of these tools.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111942675820021154",
			"parentId": "111942650943443282",
			"url": "https://tech.lgbt/@wesruv/111942675738236109",
			"author": {
				"name": "wesruv",
				"url": "https://tech.lgbt/@wesruv",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/524/352/513/111/090/original/fb58bff440e704e0.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-16T18:54:36.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>I think it's great that there are bootcamps that can train you up.</p><p>Once you get a job, dev leadership should be encouraging/training folks to broaden their skillset, but instead folks have to keep up with the churn of tooling/framework changes.</p><p>I feel like when I started we were checking to see if folks could solve problems, and understood some of the base languages that are used at a dev shop.</p><p>E.g. do you know HTML, CSS, JS, PHP, and focus on client management and troubleshooting.</p><p>A department/business might tend to use Wordpress/Drupal/Joomla, but if a project came around that wasn't a good fit, dev mgmt would do some research instead of pulling out the golden hammer.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111942678143564707",
			"parentId": "111942675820021154",
			"url": "https://tech.lgbt/@wesruv/111942678075301956",
			"author": {
				"name": "wesruv",
				"url": "https://tech.lgbt/@wesruv",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/524/352/513/111/090/original/fb58bff440e704e0.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-16T18:55:11.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <br>My TL;DR is it's classic race to the bottom, sales/marketing driven myopic nonsense.</p><p>We need speakers and evangelists that talk about the complexity, instead of evangelizing certain solutions. People that talk about pros and cons of stacks/approaches/etc.</p><p>Instead I think we have a glut of cult of personality folks that love spicy hot takes that are easier to consume, but harmful at the scale it's happening.</p><p>Also, the youths should get off my lawn, and the music is too loud now.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
